### `dispatcher-south` holds EXACTLY the rights `dispatcher-north` holds — same role, same scopes,
### same rules — and differs in one thing: the tenant its key was issued for. So every difference
### below is tenancy and can be nothing else.

### Tenant south's list of a scoped entity carries its own row and none of tenant north's seven
# @name SouthListsWorkOrders
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}

### The control: north's own list, in the same system state
# @name NorthListsWorkOrders
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

###
# @name SouthListsCustomers
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers?limit=100&order=name.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}

### A filter naming a row of the other tenant returns nothing rather than refusing — the tenant scope
### is a row predicate, so it narrows silently and cannot be probed by comparing refusals.
# @name SouthFiltersForNorthsReference
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?reference=eq.WO-1001&limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}

### Reading the other tenant's row BY ID is 404, not 403: a 403 would confirm it exists
# @name SouthReadsNorthsRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1001Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}

### The control: the same id, read by north, in the same system state
# @name NorthReadsTheSameRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1001Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### And a row id that exists in NO tenant answers identically, which is the indistinguishability
# @name SouthReadsANonexistentRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000000000fe
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}

### Modifying the other tenant's row is 404 too
# @name SouthUpdatesNorthsRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1001Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "cancelled", "priority": 5 }

###
# @name SouthDeletesNorthsRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1001Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}

### Placing a row INTO the other tenant is refused by the scope's WITH CHECK over the candidate row
# @name SouthCreatesIntoNorth
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantNorth}}",
  "reference": "WO-7001",
  "title": "Trespass",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "access_code": "TRESPASS",
  "customer_id": "{{SouthCustomerId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### Referencing the other tenant's row from a create of one's own is `unresolved-reference`, not a
### confirmation that the id exists — the reference probe runs AS THE CALLER for exactly this reason.
# @name SouthReferencesNorthsCustomer
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [422]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantSouth}}",
  "reference": "WO-7002",
  "title": "Cross-tenant reference",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "access_code": "PROBE",
  "customer_id": "{{CustomerAcmeId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### The same body naming a customer of its OWN tenant succeeds, so the 422 above is the tenant and
### not the shape of the request.
# @name SouthReferencesItsOwnCustomer
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantSouth}}",
  "reference": "WO-7003",
  "title": "Cross-tenant reference",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "access_code": "PROBE",
  "customer_id": "{{SouthCustomerId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### Asking to act in the other tenant with the tenant header is 401: a requested tenant is only ever
### a CONFIRMATION of the key's own, never a way to widen it.
# @name SouthAsksToActAsNorth
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [401]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{tenantNorth}}

### The same header naming the key's OWN tenant is honoured, so the 401 is the tenant and not the header
# @name SouthConfirmsItsOwnTenant
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
X-Alvo-Tenant: {{tenantSouth}}

### After every attempt above, tenant north's data is byte-for-byte what it was
# @name NorthIsIntact
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

###
# @name NorthsRowIsIntact
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1001Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
